thesia** **A General Anesthesia** **Definition** • General anesthesia is a drug induced state of unconsciousness with loss of sensation and reduced protective reflexes during surgery **Features** • Patient is unconscious • Pain sensation is suppressed • Protective airway reflexes are reduced • Muscle relaxation may be produced • Airway support/ventilation may be required **Uses** • Major abdominal surgery • Thoracic surgery • Neurosurgery • Major orthopaedic surgery • Procedures that require prolonged immobility --- **B Spinal Anesthesia** **Definition** • Spinal anesthesia is a regional anesthetic technique in which a local anesthetic is injected into the subarachnoid/intrathecal space producing temporary sensory and motor blockade **Features** • Patient is usually conscious • Produces numbness and weakness below the level of the block • Commonly used for lower abdominal pelvic and lower limb surgery **Common effects/complications** • Hypotension • Bradycardia • Urinary retention • Post dural puncture headache • Temporary motor weakness • Rare neurological complications --- **C Local Anesthesia** **Definition** • Local anesthesia produces temporary loss of sensation in a specific limited area without causing loss of consciousness **Examples** • Local infiltration for wound suturing • Dental anesthesia • Minor skin procedures • Peripheral nerve blocks **Mechanism** • Local anesthetics block voltage gated sodium channels preventing nerve impulse transmission --- **3 Systemic Effects of Anesthesia** **A Respiratory System** • Respiratory depression particularly with general anesthesia and sedative/opioid drugs • Reduced cough and airway prot\n<truncated 15171 bytes>\nor dominant Parkinsons disease • Medication resistant tremor when appropriate • Selected unilateral movement disorders **Complications** • Speech disturbance/dysarthria • Ataxia • Balance and gait disturbance • Weakness • Sensory disturbance • Cognitive or neurological deficits depending on the target • Hemorrhage or infection with invasive procedures --- **5 Neural Implants** **Definition** • Neural implants are medical devices implanted in or near the nervous system to record neural activity or deliver electrical stimulation to modify nervous system function **Examples** • Deep brain stimulators • Vagus nerve stimulators • Responsive neurostimulation systems • Spinal cord stimulators • Cochlear implants **Indications** • Parkinsons disease • Essential tremor • Epilepsy • Chronic neuropathic pain • Hearing loss • Selected movement disorders • Selected psychiatric or neurological conditions **Complications** • Infection • Bleeding • Device malfunction • Lead migration or breakage • Pain at the implantation site • Nerve or brain injury • Stimulation related adverse effects //////////// --- **Radiological Interpretation of OA and RA** **1 Osteoarthritis OA** • OA is a degenerative joint disease characterized by progressive cartilage loss and changes in the subchondral bone • X ray findings • Non uniform joint space narrowing • Osteophytes marginal bony outgrowths • Subchondral sclerosis • Subchondral cysts • Bony remodeling/deformity in advanced disease • Usually affects weight bearing joints such as the knee and hip • Often has an asymmetrical distribution **2 Rheumatoid Arthritis RA** • RA is a chronic autoimmune inflammatory disease affecting the synovial joints • X ray findings • Symmetrical joint space narrowing • Marginal bone erosions • Periarticular osteopenia • Soft tissue swelling • Subluxation and deformity in advanced disease • Commonly affects MCP PIP and wrist joints DIP joints are usually spared //// /////////","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Usage**\n\nI'm focusing intently on tool selection, understanding that choosing the right one is paramount. My approach is to always prioritize the appropriate tool for each specific task, and avoid unnecessary complexity. The goal is to be efficient and precise in my actions.\n\n\n**Refining Tool Selection**\n\nI'