{"step_index":5,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-26T11:30:24Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nFractures of the Upper limb —\nGeneral causes\n\n* Fall on an outstretched hand (FOOSH)\n* Direct blow or trauma\n* Road-traffic accidents\n* Sports injuries\n* Twisting or rotational injuries\n* Occupational injuries\n* Osteoporosis/pathological bone weakness\n\nGeneral clinical features\n\n* Pain and tenderness\n* Swelling and bruising\n* Deformity or abnormal movement\n* Loss of function and restricted ROM\n* Crepitus\n* Shortening or abnormal alignment\n* Neurovascular deficit in complicated fractures\n\nGeneral complications\n\n* Neurovascular injury\n* Compartment syndrome\n* Infection in open fractures\n* Delayed union/non-union\n* Malunion\n* Joint stiffness\n* Post-traumatic arthritis\n* Complex regional pain syndrome\n\n⸻\n\n1. Fractures of Clavicle and Scapula\n\nA. Fracture of clavicle\n\nDefinition:\nFracture of the clavicle, most commonly involving the middle third.\n\nCauses\n\n* Fall directly on the shoulder\n* Fall on an outstretched hand\n* Direct blow to clavicle\n* Road-traffic or sports injury\n\nClinical features\n\n* Pain and tenderness over clavicle\n* Swelling and deformity\n* Patient supports the affected arm\n* Shoulder drooping forwards and downwards\n* Abnormal mobility/crepitus\n* Difficulty in shoulder movements\n* Check distal neurovascular status\n\nComplications\n\n* Malunion\n* Non-union\n* Neurovascular injury\n* Skin perforation\n* Brachial plexus injury\n* Pneumothorax\n* Shoulder stiffness\n\nConservative management\n\n* Arm sling or figure-of-eight brace\n* Analgesics\n* Ice/elevation initially\n* Early elbow, wrist and hand movements\n* Gradual shoulder ROM after pain subsides\n\nSurgical management\n\n* ORIF with plate and screws\n* Indications: open fracture, neurovascular injury, significant displacement/shortening, threatened skin, or selected unstable displaced fractures.\n\nB. Fracture of scapula\n\nCauses\n\n* Usually high-energy trauma\n* Direct blow\n* RTA\n* Fall from height\n* Indirect injury through shoulder\n\nClinical features\n\n* Severe shoulder/scapular pain\n* Swelling and tenderness\n* Painful shoulder movements\n* Reduced should\n<truncated 78120 bytes>\nnjuries above approximately T6. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n7. Clinical Patterns of Incomplete SCI\r\n\r\nA. Central Cord Syndrome\r\n\r\n* Usually follows cervical hyperextension injury.\r\n* Upper limbs are affected more than lower limbs.\r\n* Hand weakness may be prominent.\r\n* Sensory disturbance may occur.\r\n\r\nB. Brown-Séquard Syndrome\r\n\r\nDue to hemisection of the spinal cord:\r\n\r\n* Ipsilateral motor weakness\r\n* Ipsilateral loss of proprioception/vibration\r\n* Contralateral loss of pain and temperature\r\n\r\nC. Anterior Cord Syndrome\r\n\r\n* Motor paralysis below the lesion\r\n* Loss of pain and temperature\r\n* Relative preservation of dorsal-column functions such as vibration and proprioception. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n8. Complete and Incomplete Injury\r\n\r\nComplete SCI\r\n\r\n* No sensory or motor function is preserved in the sacral segments S4–S5.\r\n* No sacral sparing.\r\n* Classified as AIS/ASIA Grade A. \r\n\r\nIncomplete SCI\r\n\r\n* Some sensory and/or motor function is preserved below the neurological level, including sacral function.\r\n* Classified as AIS B–D depending on the amount of preserved function.\r\n* AIS E: normal motor and sensory examination. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n9. Clinical Examination\r\n\r\nAssessment should include:\r\n\r\n* Level of neurological injury\r\n* Motor power\r\n* Sensory examination\r\n* Deep tendon reflexes\r\n* Anal sensation and voluntary anal contraction\r\n* Muscle tone\r\n* Respiratory function\r\n* Bladder and bowel function\r\n* Autonomic function\r\n\r\nThe International Standards for Neurological Classification of Spinal Cord Injury (ISNCSCI/ASIA examination) is used to determine the neurological level and severity of injury. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n10. Important Complications\r\n\r\n* Respiratory failure\r\n* Neurogenic shock\r\n* Spinal shock\r\n* Pressure sores\r\n* Deep-vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism\r\n* Urinary tract infection\r\n* Neurogenic bladder and bowel\r\n* Contractures\r\n* Spasticity\r\n* Autonomic dysreflexia\r\n* Chronic pain\r\n* Permanent paralysis/////////////////\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-08-26T17:00:24+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>","truncated_fields":["content"]}
