{"step_index":11,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-26T11:47:44Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nFractures of Thoracic and Lumbar Spine —\n\nDefinition\n\nA thoracic or lumbar spine fracture is a break in one or more vertebral structures of the thoracic or lumbar spine, with or without associated ligament, spinal cord or nerve-root injury.\n\nThoracolumbar fractures commonly occur around the T11-L2 region, which represents the transition between the relatively rigid thoracic spine and mobile lumbar spine. \n\n⸻\n\n1. Mechanism of Injury\n\nThoracolumbar fractures commonly result from:\n\n1. Flexion-compression\n\n* Sudden forward flexion of the spine.\n* Produces compression/wedge fractures.\n* Common after falls and road-traffic accidents.\n\n2. Axial compression\n\n* Vertical force transmitted through the spine.\n* Produces burst fractures with compression of the vertebral body and po…\nFRACTURE OF RIB CAGE —\n\nDefinition\n\nA fracture of the rib cage is a break in the continuity of one or more ribs or the sternum, usually caused by direct or indirect trauma. Severe chest trauma may be associated with pneumothorax, haemothorax, pulmonary contusion or flail chest.\n\n⸻\n\n1. FRACTURE OF RIBS\n\nMechanism of Injury\n\n* Direct trauma: A blow to the chest causes the rib to bend and fracture at the site of impact.\n* Indirect trauma: Compression of the chest from the sides may fracture ribs at their point of maximum curvature.\n* RTA\n* Fall\n* Sports injury\n* Crush injury\n* Severe coughing may rarely cause stress fractures.\n\nClinical Features\n\n* Severe localized chest pain\n* Pain aggravated by:\n    * Deep inspiration\n    * Coughing\n    * Sneezing\n    * Movement\n* Local …\nINFLAMMATION AND REPAIR —-\n\nIntroduction to Soft-Tissue Healing\n\nDefinition of Inflammation\n\nInflammation is a protective response of living vascularized tissue to injury, infection or irritation, which removes the injurious stimulus and